# What never enters the build context.
#
# The context is packed and uploaded before a single instruction runs — to the local
# daemon on `docker build`, and to Cloud Build on `gcloud run deploy --source .`. The
# tracked repository is a few megabytes; `node_modules/` on its own is several hundred.
# So this file is most of the difference between a deploy that takes a minute and one
# that takes twenty.
#
# Two things it is NOT. It does not decide what ends up in the image — the runner stage
# copies five paths by name and ignores everything else. And it does not control what
# `gcloud` uploads: that is `.gcloudignore`, which gcloud derives from `.gitignore` when
# no `.gcloudignore` exists. See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md.


# --- rebuilt inside the image, never copied in ---------------------------------------
#
# `npm ci` and `next build` produce both of these in the `deps` and `builder` stages.
# Excluding node_modules is also what stops `COPY . .` overwriting the tree that `deps`
# resolved: a host `node_modules` from Windows or macOS carries native binaries for the
# wrong platform, and the result is an image that builds cleanly and crashes on boot.
node_modules
.next
out
dist
build

# --- history, not source -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Megabytes of packfile that no build step reads.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github

# --- secrets -------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The Dockerfile passes no key at any stage and no ARG accepts one; this is the second
# lock rather than the first, and it is load-bearing.
#
# Verified, not assumed: a local `next build` with a `.env` present copies that file
# into `.next/standalone/.env`. The runner stage copies the whole standalone directory,
# so a `.env` left in the build context would put six live Gemini keys inside a public
# image as a plain file. This line is what stops that.
.env
.env.*

# --- test and coverage artefacts -------------------------------------------------------
#
# The runner copies none of them and no request reads them. CI is where a failing test
# stops a release — not a deploy that has already uploaded its context and paid for a
# builder.
#
# Worth knowing rather than discovering: `next build` type-checks against tsconfig.json,
# whose `include` is `**/*.ts`, so it does compile `tests/` when they are present.
# Excluding them here means the image build checks a smaller set than CI does. That is
# the intended direction — CI is the gate and runs the whole suite; the image build's job
# is to produce an image — but it does mean a green `docker build` is not a green CI.
tests
coverage
vitest.config.mts

# --- research working files, never product ---------------------------------------------
.planning

# --- sample legal documents ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Kept. ~84 KB, and the server reads it on every request for a sample report.
#
# It used to be excluded, on the reasoning that `fixtures/` was recorder-only and no
# request could reach it. That stopped being true when the samples started rendering
# through the real adapter instead of through hand-built view objects: a sample report
# now quotes its own source text, and the source is here. Excluding it would 404 every
# sample in production while every sample kept working locally.
#
# `golden/reports/` is the analysis, `fixtures/` is the document it was analysing.
# Neither is useful alone, so they are shipped together.

# --- prose ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Read by people, on GitHub. LICENSE has no extension and is kept.
docs
*.md

# --- build cache and editor state --------------------------------------------------------
*.tsbuildinfo
.eslintcache
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- this build's own inputs -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Docker reads the Dockerfile separately, so excluding it here is safe and means editing
# a comment in it cannot invalidate the `npm ci` layer.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
